{*
   aLatteX Demo: Extends, article - the deep end of the chain.

       base.latte  <-  base-article.latte  <-  this record

   Three levels, and nothing here knows about base.latte. It names its parent
   and fills the blocks that parent declared; the grandparent's markup arrives
   because base-article.latte extends it in turn. Latte resolves the chain at
   render time, loading each template separately - which is why editing
   base.latte changes this page without anything else being touched, and why
   Tracy's aLatteX panel shows all three with the relation that pulled each in.

   {include parent} appears twice on this page: once in base-article.latte's
   crumbs, and once below, where the child adds to the parent's aside rather
   than replacing it.
*}
{extends 'base-article.latte'}

{block title}{$pagetitle} (three levels){/block}

{block bodyClass}alx-base alx-article alx-deep{/block}

{block standfirst}
    A template three levels deep, and the middle one is a file too.
{/block}

{block body}
    <p>
        The chain is <code>base.latte</code> &rarr;
        <code>base-article.latte</code> &rarr; this template. Each level only
        names the one above it.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>The doctype, header and footer come from <code>base.latte</code>.</li>
        <li>The article frame comes from <code>base-article.latte</code>.</li>
        <li>This text, and the aside below, come from the database.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        A chunk partial still works at this depth, on top of the two layouts:
    </p>
    {include 'chunk:aLatteXDemoPartial', title: 'Included three levels down', items: ['base', 'base-article', 'this template']}

    <div class="alx-content">[*content*]</div>
{/block}

{block asideExtra}
    <p>
        Added by the child through <code>&#123;block asideExtra&#125;</code>,
        which base-article.latte left empty for exactly this.
    </p>
{/block}
